From: Marco Martins
Subject: b43: start the beacons at the next TBTT from the TSF

b43_set_beacon_int() sets the first TBTT to one beacon interval, as if
the TSF were near zero. The hardware compares it with the low 32 bits of
the TSF. While those are less than 2^31 us past it, it moves it on by
whole intervals until it is ahead. Further past, it takes it for a time
still to come and sends no beacon at all: on a BCM5352 the start stayed
at one interval while the TSF ran on. Without beacons there are no
DTIMs, so the broadcasts b43 holds for stations in power save are never
sent. The multicast ring fills, and b43 stops the mac80211 queue of the
frame that filled it, best effort for broadcasts, so all best-effort
traffic stops: a station still associates, but gets no DHCP lease.

The TSF is not reset when an interface comes up. It keeps counting for
as long as the hardware runs, and a station interface on the same radio
synchronises it to its AP's, which can be any value. So an access point
brought up in the second half of any 2^32 us (71.6 minutes) of the TSF
sends no beacons, as after a restart beside a station interface.

Where the hardware does catch up, the beacons go out at multiples of
the interval in the low 32 bits only. 2^32 us is not a multiple of an
interval (it is 4 TU more than one of 100 TU), so once the TSF is past
2^32 us, they are off the TBTTs, where the TSF is a multiple of the
interval, by the upper 32 bits of the TSF times 4 TU, modulo the
interval. Stations in power save wake up at the TBTTs for the beacon.

Start at the next TBTT from the TSF instead, as ath5k does.
---
--- a/drivers/net/wireless/broadcom/b43/main.c
+++ b/drivers/net/wireless/broadcom/b43/main.c
@@ -1856,10 +1856,22 @@

 static void b43_set_beacon_int(struct b43_wldev *dev, u16 beacon_int)
 {
+	u64 tsf;
+	u32 rem;
+
 	b43_time_lock(dev);
 	if (dev->dev->core_rev >= 3) {
+		/* Start at the next TBTT, the next multiple of the beacon
+		 * interval on the TSF. The TSF need not be near zero: it keeps
+		 * counting while the interface is down, and a station interface
+		 * on the radio synchronises it to its AP. The hardware sends no
+		 * beacon for a start more than 2^31 us behind the TSF. */
+		b43_tsf_read(dev, &tsf);
+		tsf >>= 10;
+		div_u64_rem(tsf, beacon_int, &rem);
+		tsf += beacon_int - rem;
 		b43_write32(dev, B43_MMIO_TSF_CFP_REP, (beacon_int << 16));
-		b43_write32(dev, B43_MMIO_TSF_CFP_START, (beacon_int << 10));
+		b43_write32(dev, B43_MMIO_TSF_CFP_START, lower_32_bits(tsf << 10));
 	} else {
 		b43_write16(dev, 0x606, (beacon_int >> 6));
 		b43_write16(dev, 0x610, beacon_int);
